  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Friday...\n\nDry high pressure aloft will keep the area hot & dry through the \nweekend. The main issues remain fire weather and smoke impacts. \nRegarding fire weather, lighter/easterly winds today will keep \nfire conditions a bit relaxed from yesterday (though downslope \nwinds remain somewhat breezy this afternoon along the far western \nfoothills). Saturday is looking hotter & drier (near 100F, single\ndigit RH) with winds shifting to the north and becoming deeply- \nmixed/breezy. Expect widespread wind gusts of 20-30 mph, up to 35 \nmph along western foothills. Have upgraded the Fire Weather Watch \nto a Red Flag Warning from 11am to 9pm, and have added eastern \nzone 133. Please be careful not to spark a fire. Sunday's setup \nlooks similar to Saturday but with slightly less wind, cooler \ntemps and higher humidities. Something to monitor.\n\nMonsoon moisture rotating around the 4-corners high will approach\nsouth central MT tomorrow. This high level moisture will probably\nresult in some diurnal buildups over the Beartooth-Absarokas with\nperhaps virga, but the lower air mass will remain quite dry (as \nalready mentioned). Pwats begin to climb Sunday, especially in the\neast, and by evening a weak shortwave w/ a southeasterly low \nlevel jet may produce a few t-storms near the Dakotas border.\n\nPwats continue to rise next week, mainly due to plains moisture \nbut we should see a bit of an increase in monsoon moisture as \nwell. Pwats expected to reach an inch in our east by Monday, and \nmay get near an inch as far west as Billings by midweek. The \ngreater moisture/instability along with a series of weak \nshortwaves from the west will bring increasing thunderstorm \npotential, initially in the east but eventually including the \nentire cwa by Wednesday or Thursday. A stronger wave dropping out \nof southwest Canada looks to bring a greater chance of showers & \nthunderstorms along with cooler temps (highs in 70s to lower 80s) \nThursday and Friday. Expect temps in the 80s to lower 90s Monday \nto Wednesday. Generally speaking, this transition to a more humid \nregime will be beneficial with regard to fire weather conditions, \nbut the increasing lightning activity may very well cause new fire\nstarts after the current stretch of hot & dry days. Most of the \nregion has not yet seen measurable rain in August.\n\nJKL",
